    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Jains hold that Mahavira, born a prince, abandoned royal life at about thirty, practised severe asceticism for twelve and a half years, attained Kevala Jnana, and then preached for thirty years.</li><li>He taught five vows, non-violence first among them, and a doctrine of many-sided reality, and Jains hold that his chief disciple Gautama Swami compiled his teachings as the Agamas.</li><li>Scholars agree that he lived, but they disagree about his dates, and they see him as a reformer of an older Jain or proto-Jain community led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a>, not as its founder.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1974, for the 2,500th anniversary of Mahavira's nirvana, the mendicants of the Śvētāmbara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> and Sthānakavāsī sects for the first time assembled on one platform and agreed on a common flag and emblem.</p></aside>
    </section>

