    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">599 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Traditional date of Mahavira's birth at Kundagrama, as both Jain sects hold</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">At about thirty</span><span class="rf-text">He abandons royal life and becomes a naked ascetic, according to the Ācārāṅga Sūtra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">After twelve and a half years</span><span class="rf-text">He attains Kevala Jnana under a Sāla tree on the bank of the Rijuvalika River</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">After his omniscience</span><span class="rf-text">He initiates his first disciples, the eleven Ganadharas, led by Indrabhuti Gautama</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">527 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Śvētāmbara date of his nirvana at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavapuri">Pawapuri</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a> say 510 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1st century BCE to 2nd century CE</span><span class="rf-text">The earliest known images of him are made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 550 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The Akota hoard's bronze of Mahavira as Jivantasvami, the earliest known clothed Jain idol, is dated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1974</span><span class="rf-text">Jain sects hold a joint commemoration of the 2,500th anniversary of his nirvana</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parshvanatha.jpg" alt="Parshvanatha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jirawala_Parshwanath.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fawks3107, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mahavira, whose birth name was Vardhamana, is placed by Jains in a long line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankaras</a>, ford-makers who found a passage across the sea of birth and death. He is the 24th and last of the present time cycle. Jain texts describe 26 earlier births, in which he was among other things a denizen of hell, a lion, a god, and Marichi, the son of Bharata Chakravartin.</p><p>Jain tradition says he was born in 599 BCE at Kundagrama in the Kingdom of the Videhas, to King Siddhartha of the Nāya tribe and Queen Trishala. The Nāyas were kshatriyas, and the Ācārāṅga Sūtra calls Mahavira a prince of Videha. Where Kundagrama lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> is disputed: J. P. Sharma identified it as a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>, and another candidate is the village of Basu Kund, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</p><p>The Śvetāmbara texts tell that his embryo first formed in a Bamana woman and was moved to Trishala's womb, a story the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a> do not accept. The two traditions also differ about his marriage. The Digambaras say he refused to marry Yashoda, while the Śvetāmbaras say he married her and had a daughter, Priyadarshana. His parents were, according to the Ācārāṅga Sūtra, lay followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mahavira&#8221; cites where it covers Birthplace, Previous births, Birth, Early life:</p><ul><li>Paul Dundas, The Jains (Second ed.) (Routledge, 2002)</li><li>Helmuth von Glasenapp, Jainism: An Indian Religion of Salvation (Motilal Banarsidass Publishing House, 1999)</li><li>Bron Taylor, Encyclopedia of Religion and Nature (Bloomsbury Academic, 2008)</li><li>Margaret A. Mills (ed.), Kalpa Sutra (by Jerome Bauer) in South Asian Folklore: An Encyclopedia (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra.jpg" alt="Kalpa Sūtra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra">Kalpa Sūtra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalpasutra_Mahavira_Nirvana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At about thirty, some accounts say twenty-eight, Mahavira left his family and royal life. He undertook severe fasts and bodily mortifications, meditated under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> tree and discarded his clothes. The Ācārāṅga Sūtra gives a graphic description of his hardships.</p><p>According to traditional accounts, he attained Kevala Jnana, omniscience, under a Sāla tree on the bank of the River Rijuvalika near Jrimbhikagrama, after twelve years of rigorous penance, at the age of 43. The Śvētāmbara tradition says he then travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to teach for thirty years, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a> believe he stayed in his Samavasarana, the holy assembly, and delivered sermons there.</p><p>Jain texts name eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a> as his first disciples, the eleven Ganadharas, led by Indrabhuti Gautama. Mahavira initiated his mendicants with the five vows. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra">Kalpa Sūtra</a>, he had 14,000 male ascetics, 36,000 female ascetics, 159,000 male lay followers and 318,000 female lay followers. Tradition names Srenika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Kunika</a>, popularly known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> and Ajatashatru, and Chetaka of Videha as royal followers, and holds Chandana to be the leader of the female monastic order.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/digambara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/digambara.jpg" alt="Digambara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahavir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dayodaya, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mahavira is best remembered for teaching that ahimsa, non-violence, is the supreme moral virtue. It applies to actions, speech and thought, and extends to every living being, since injuring any being creates bad karma. The Ācārāṅga Sūtra says that he saw life in animals, plants, insects, water, fire and wind, and taught that a monk should not light a fire or wave his arms in the air, lest he injure beings in those states of matter.</p><p>With it, the Jain Agamas list four more vows that ascetics and householders observe: satya, truthfulness; asteya, non-stealing; brahmacharya, chastity for monks and faithfulness for householders; and aparigraha, non-attachment, which for mendicants means owning nothing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> sky-clad ascetics read nakedness as the sign of that vow.</p><p>He taught that the soul exists, is substantial and eternal, and is bound to samsara by karma, that there is no creator deity, and that liberation is reached by self-awareness, self-cultivation and restraint. He also taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anekantavada">anekantavada</a>, many-sided reality: truth has many aspects, and language expresses only one perspective at a time. Jain tradition holds that his teachings were compiled by Gautama Swami in twelve parts.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mahavira&#8221; cites where it covers Teachings, Agamas, Five Vows, Soul:</p><ul><li>Paul Dundas, The Jains (Second ed.) (Routledge, 2002)</li><li>Samani Charitrapragya, Ahimsā, Anekānta, and Jaininsm (Motilal Banarsidass Publishing House, 2004)</li><li>Jeffery D. Long, Jainism: An Introduction (I. B. Tauris, 2009)</li><li>Moriz Winternitz, History of Indian Literature: Buddhist &amp; Jain Literature (Motilal Banarsidass Publishing House, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha.jpg" alt="Gautama Buddha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_(Dhammajak_Mutra).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">พระมหาเทวประภาส วชิรญาณเมธี (ผู้ถ่าย-ปล่อยสัญญาอนุญาตภาพให้นำไปใช้ได้เพื่อการศึกษาโดยอยู่ภา่ยใต้ cc-by-sa-3.0) …, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mahavira taught among rival traditions. Early Buddhist texts mention the Niganthas, the community he is thought to have reformed, and Buddhist sources make him a contemporary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>, in the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>. Each religion of the time was highly critical of the knowledge systems and ideologies of its rivals. Colonial-era Indologists took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> for a sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> because of similar iconography and ascetic practice. Later scholarship found the teachings different: Winternitz notes that Mahavira taught a very elaborate belief in the soul, which Buddhists denied.</p><p>Later debates within Jainism also look back to him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a> hold that a woman cannot gain liberation through asceticism and can at best be reborn as a man, while the Śvetāmbaras read him as encouraging both sexes to pursue moksha. The two sects differ on where he preached, on his marriage, and on the embryo transfer.</p><p>Historians doubt the traditional chronology. The Vira Nirvana Samvat count rests on a list of kings that Rapson calls unreliable, as it confuses rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and other kingdoms. Paul Dundas also cautions that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anekantavada">anekantavada</a> has been misread as general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>: it concerns the nature of reality, and the five vows for monks and nuns carry no 'perhaps'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tirthankara.jpg" alt="Tirthankara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jain_24-Tirthankaras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jain texts place his nirvana at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavapuri">Pawapuri</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. The Śvētāmbara tradition dates it to 527 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> tradition to 510 BCE, and in both his jiva is believed to dwell in Siddhashila, the home of liberated souls. His chief disciple, Gautama, is said to have attained omniscience the night Mahavira reached nirvana.</p><p>Accounts differ. Some texts describe a simple nirvana, and others recount grand celebrations attended by gods and kings. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a>'s Mahapurana heavenly beings arrive to perform the funeral rites. Some texts say that at the age of 72 he gave his last preaching over six days to a large crowd, which fell asleep and woke to find him gone, leaving only his nails and hair, which his followers cremated. The Digambara Pravachanasara holds that only the nails and hair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankaras</a> remain, and the rest of the body dissolves in the air like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>.</p><p>Historians date his death differently. On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>'s short chronology, Dundas suggests that Mahavira may have died around 425 BCE or a few years after, and Long suggests a lifespan of c. 499 to 427 BCE. Others, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a>'s chronologies, give 549 BCE for his birth and 477 or c. 468 to 467 BCE for his death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mahavira's legacy is the codification of Jain philosophy and the four-fold order of monks, nuns, laymen and laywomen, which gave both ascetics and householders a path and let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> survive as a living tradition. Jain tradition says that the teachings were gradually lost after about 300 BCE, when a famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> dispersed the monks. Later efforts to gather the canon, including an attempt in the 5th century CE, did not reconcile the Śvetāmbara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> versions, and each tradition holds a somewhat different and incomplete one. The texts were first written on palm leaf: the Ṣaṭkhaṅḍāgama, among the oldest Digambara texts, was written down under Āchārya Dharasena.</p><p>He is among the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankaras</a> who attract the most devotional worship. His emblem is the lion, and his earliest images come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. Jains celebrate his birth as Mahavir Janma Kalyanak and his nirvana as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, which marks the Jain New Year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavapuri">Pawapuri</a>, with its Jal Mandir, is the chief pilgrimage site.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> called Mahavira the greatest authority on ahimsa, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> wrote that he proclaimed that religion is a reality and not a mere social convention.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mahavira, called Vardhamana at birth, is held by Jains to be the 24th and last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>. He taught five vows, with ahimsa first, and scholars see him as a reformer of an older Jain community, with dates that remain disputed.</em></p>

